      "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nAdd a tuned Triton fused-MoE configuration for **NVIDIA A100-SXM4-40GB** covering the K-EXAONE-236B-A23B MoE shape at tensor parallel size 16 in bfloat16:\n\n- `E=128` experts, `moe_intermediate_size=2048`, so `N=128` per rank at TP=16\n- file: `vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/configs/E=128,N=128,device_name=NVIDIA_A100-SXM4-40GB.json`\n\nThere is no A100-40GB entry for any shape today (36 A100 files exist, all `A100-SXM4-80GB`), so bf16 K-EXAONE on 16× A100-40GB falls back to the default heuristic config.\n\n## Claims\n\n- Fused-MoE kernel time drops at every batch size M in {1 … 4096}; see the table below.\n- End to end (bf16, TP16, MTP, prefix caching, `--max-num-batched-tokens 4096`): output throughput +49% at concurrency 16 and +15% at concurrency 32, with single-stream latency unchanged.\n- Configuration-only change: no model code or numerics are touched. gsm8k accuracy and MTP acceptance are unchanged.\n\n## Validation\n\n**Tuning** (16× A100-SXM4-40GB host, vLLM main @ c41b2639e2, Triton 3.7.1, Ray over 8 GPUs):\n\n```bash\npython3 benchmarks/kernels/benchmark_moe.py --model LGAI-EXAONE/K-EXAONE-236B-A23B \\\n  --tp-size 16 --dtype auto --tune --batch-size 1 2 4 8 16 32 64 128 256 512 1024 2048 4096\n```\n\n**Kernel time** (`benchmark_moe.py` benchmark mode, one A100-40GB, bf16, TP=16 shape; default = file absent):\n\n| M (tokens) | default (µs) | tuned (µs) | change |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 71.65 | 62.91 | -12.2% |\n| 2 | 98.36 | 93.73 | -4.7% |\n| 4 | 146.36 | 128.84 | -12.0% |\n| 8 | 226.62 | 210.34 | -7.2% |\n| 16 | 348.89 | 311.62 | -10.7% |\n| 32 | 468.12 | 413.83 | -11.6% |\n| 64 | 492.68 | 462.95 | -6.0% |\n| 128 | 526.97 | 485.47 | -7.9% |\n| 256 | 568.08 | 516.27 | -9.1% |\n| 512 | 615.84 | 599.85 | -2.6% |\n| 1024 | 880.61 | 723.03 | -17.9% |\n| 2048 | 1255.63 | 1044.73 | -16.8% |\n| 4096 | 2026.12 | 1669.79 | -17.6% |\n\nThe tuned config wins at all 13 batch sizes; the sum of kernel times drops from 7726 µs to 6723 µs (−13.0%), median change −9.1%.\n\n**End to end** (`vllm serve` bf16 TP=16, `--speculative-config '{\"method\":\"mtp\",\"num_speculative_tokens\":1}'`, prefix caching on, `--max-num-batched-tokens 4096`; `vllm bench serve --dataset-name random --random-input-len 1024 --random-output-len 512 --ignore-eos`):\n\n| | default config | tuned config |\n|---|---|---|\n| conc 1: TPOT | 8.53 ms | 8.61 ms |\n| conc 16: output tok/s | 512 | **763** (+49%) |\n| conc 32: output tok/s | 1144 | **1310** (+15%) |\n| single stream, real prompts (gsm8k, greedy) | 118 tok/s | 120 tok/s |\n| gsm8k-200 accuracy / MTP acceptance | 0.92 / 0.917 | 0.92 / 0.895–0.917 (run noise) |\n\n- `jq empty` on the JSON passes; `pre-commit` passes.\n- The same procedure for the TP=8 shape (`N=256`) is only relevant to int8 on 40GB (bf16 does not fit at TP=8) and will follow in a separate PR together with `int8_w8a8` support in `benchmark_moe.py`.\n\n## Details\n\nThe config was produced with the standard tuning script over the batch sizes above (the small sizes 1/2/4 were tuned in a second pass and merged, since decode steps with MTP run at M ≤ 8 and the first pass had left them on the M=8 entry). Keys follow the existing A100-80GB files.\n\nDuplicate-work check: no open PR touches A100-40GB configs or this shape (searched `E=128,N=128` and `A100-SXM4-40GB` in open PRs).\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [x] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\nAI assistance was used while preparing this PR; I reviewed every changed line and ran all of the validation above myself.\n\n",
